=====================================================================
PROBE 3a  NEWTON-SKELETON OBSTRUCTION TO r = (0,0)
          Every member of the frozen skeleton has y1 in x1^3*k[x1,x2]
          and y2 in x1^2*k[x1,x2].  Then every term of {y1,y2} carries
          x1-degree >= 4, so the bracket is divisible by x1^4 for the
          WHOLE deformation space, not just at the near-miss.
          Verified below on generic members of the skeleton.
=====================================================================
   generic skeleton member: {y1,y2} / x1^4 is a polynomial : 1
   constant term of {y1,y2} (the Keller target) : 0
   => on the frozen skeleton the Keller condition {y1,y2} = const != 0
      is not merely obstructed to first order, it is UNREACHABLE:
      the constant term vanishes identically on the whole box.
      Any genuine twin must therefore CHANGE the x1-support, i.e.
      change the combinatorial type -- which is exactly the chain
      layer that probes 1 and 2 govern.

=====================================================================
PROBE 3b  ADMISSIBLE CHAIN DEGREES AND THEIR RIGIDITY
          N = 2a = 3b+1 forces b odd and D = N-3 = 3b-2, so the
          skeleton exists only for D = 1 (mod 6).  Parameter count:
          a+b monic coefficients against N-4 cancellation equations,
          with a 1-dimensional scaling w -> mu*w always acting.
=====================================================================
     D  | deg p | deg r | Belyi N | unknowns | equations | expected dim
   1   |   2   |   1   |    4   |    3     |     0     |    3
   7   |   5   |   3   |    10   |    8     |     6     |    2
   13   |   8   |   5   |    16   |    13     |     12     |    1
   19   |   11   |   7   |    22   |    18     |     18     |    0
   25   |   14   |   9   |    28   |    23     |     24     |    -1
   31   |   17   |   11   |    34   |    28     |     30     |    -2
   37   |   20   |   13   |    40   |    33     |     36     |    -3

   Rigidity (solution set = the scaling orbit only) needs
   unknowns - equations = 1, i.e. (5D+13)/6 = D, i.e. D = 13 EXACTLY.
   D < 13 is under-determined (positive-dimensional twin families);
   D > 13 is over-determined (expected empty).

=====================================================================
PROBE 3c  EXPLICIT TWIN CENSUS - solving deg(p^2 - w r^3) = 3 for the
          small admissible degrees.  The origin p = w^a, r = w^b is a
          degenerate solution of every instance (it makes p^2 - w r^3
          vanish identically), so it is saturated away by requiring
          p(0) != 0 and r(0) != 0.  Dimension is reported in the
          parameter space; the scaling orbit w -> mu*w accounts for 1.
=====================================================================
   D = 1  (deg p = 2, deg r = 1):  #equations = 0   parameter dim = 3   moduli after scaling = 2
   D = 7  (deg p = 5, deg r = 3):  #equations = 6   parameter dim = 2   moduli after scaling = 1

   D = 13 (deg p = 8, deg r = 5):  #equations = 12  parameter dim = 1  moduli after scaling = 0   [recorded; set RUN_D13 = 1 to recompute, ~10 min]

  READING.
  * D = 1 and D = 7 carry positive-dimensional twin families: the
    skeleton is realizable there but far too soft to pin a framework.
  * D = 13 is the unique rigid degree.  Borisov's chain degree is not
    an artefact of his search - it is forced by (5D+13)/6 = D.
  * D >= 19 is over-determined.
  * D = 4 -- the ONLY chain degree at which the probe-2 trapdoor is
    self-consistent -- is not 1 mod 6 and so does not admit the
    skeleton at all.  The trapdoor has no twin to walk through it.
